# WDDM Paging Collapse Simulator

## TL;DR

When a Windows GPU's physical VRAM is exceeded, the WDDM driver silently
pages memory to system RAM over PCIe.  This benchmark **quantifies** the
resulting performance collapse and provides empirical evidence that LLM
runtimes (vLLM, TGI, etc.) need **strict Admission Control** at the
physical-VRAM boundary — not at the `torch.cuda.memory_reserved()`
boundary.

**Measured on an RTX 5060 (8 GB): 68.4× latency collapse, 9.13 → 0.13 TFLOPS.**

## The Physics

| Resource | Bandwidth | Latency |
|----------|-----------|---------|
| GDDR7 VRAM (RTX 5060) | ~448 GB/s | ~10 ns |
| PCIe Gen4 x16 | ~32 GB/s | ~1 µs |
| DDR5 system RAM | ~50 GB/s | ~80 ns |

When WDDM pages a 64 KB GPU page to system RAM, every subsequent access
to that page incurs a PCIe round-trip.  For an LLM forward pass that
streams model weights from VRAM, this turns a memory-bandwidth-bound
kernel into a PCIe-bandwidth-bound kernel.  The theoretical bandwidth
ratio alone predicts ~14× slowdown; real-world page-fault overhead,
TLB thrashing, and PCIe contention push it far higher.

## Experiment Design

```
Phase 1 (Baseline)          Phase 2 (Stress)
┌──────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────┐
│ Filler: 50% VRAM │        │ Filler: 115% VRAM│ ← WDDM pages to RAM
│ GEMM 2048×2048   │        │ GEMM 2048×2048   │ ← page faults on access
│ → 9.13 TFLOPS    │        │ → 0.13 TFLOPS    │
└──────────────────┘        └──────────────────┘
```

1. **Baseline** — Fill VRAM to 50 %, run a GEMM benchmark.
2. **Stress** — Fill VRAM to 115 % of physical capacity, forcing WDDM
   to evict pages to system RAM.  Re-run the same GEMM.
3. **Compare** — Report mean / P50 / P99 latency and TFLOPS collapse.

## Actual Results (RTX 5060, 8 GB, WSL2 / Windows 11)

```
==============================================================
   WDDM PAGING COLLAPSE SIMULATOR
==============================================================
  GPU           NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060
  VRAM          7.93 GB  (limit used: 7.93 GB)
  GEMM          2048x2048  float32  (17.2 GFLOP/iter)
  Iterations    50
  PyTorch       2.13.0+cu130   CUDA 13.0
==============================================================

==============================================================
  PHASE 1 - BASELINE  (fill ~4.0 GB / 50% of VRAM)
==============================================================
  VRAM [after fill]:  allocated=4.00 GB  reserved=4.00 GB  physical=7.93 GB

  -- Baseline --
  Mean :     1.88 ms
  P50  :     1.56 ms
  P99  :     3.01 ms
  Min  :     1.37 ms
  Max  :     6.53 ms
  Std  :     0.82 ms
  TFLOPS (mean): 9.13

==============================================================
  PHASE 2 - STRESS  (fill ~9.1 GB / 115% of VRAM -> force paging)
==============================================================
  VRAM [after oversubscribe]:  allocated=9.28 GB  reserved=9.28 GB  physical=7.93 GB

  -- Stress (paged) --
  Mean :   128.72 ms
  P50  :   124.72 ms
  P99  :   169.49 ms
  Min  :   102.15 ms
  Max  :   171.16 ms
  Std  :    15.04 ms
  TFLOPS (mean): 0.13

==============================================================
  RESULTS
==============================================================
  Mean latency collapse      68.4x slower
  P99  latency collapse      56.3x slower
  TFLOPS collapse            68.4x lower
```

### Why 68× and not the theoretical 14×?

The naive bandwidth ratio (448 / 32 ≈ 14×) underestimates the real
collapse because it ignores:

1. **Page-fault overhead** — each access to an evicted page triggers a
   GPU trap → WDDM scheduler → DMA copy over PCIe → TLB re-map → resume.
   This fixed cost dominates for small, random access patterns.
2. **TLB thrashing** — the GPU's translation lookaside buffer cannot
   keep up with pages being constantly evicted and re-fetched.
3. **PCIe contention** — the display compositor (DWM) shares the same
   PCIe link, further reducing effective bandwidth for the compute
   workload.
4. **Variance explosion** — standard deviation went from 0.82 ms to
   15.04 ms (18×), indicating unpredictable page-fault storms that
   are catastrophic for LLM tail latency (P99).

## Requirements

| Component | Minimum |
|-----------|---------|
| GPU | NVIDIA, ≥ 6 GB VRAM (tested: RTX 5060 8 GB) |
| OS | Windows 10 21H2+ or Windows 11 (native recommended) |
| Driver | NVIDIA Game Ready / Studio ≥ 530.x |
| Python | 3.10+ |
| PyTorch | 2.1+ with CUDA support |

> **WSL2 note:** This benchmark was validated on WSL2 (Ubuntu 22.04)
> with CUDA 13.0 passthrough.  The WDDM driver on the Windows host
> allowed oversubscription and paging occurred as expected.  Behaviour
> may vary with different driver versions; if you see OOM instead of
> slowdown, run natively on Windows.

## Quick Start

```bash
git clone https://github.com/JohnScheuer/wddm-paging-simulator.git
cd wddm-paging-simulator
pip install -r requirements.txt
python simulate_collapse.py
```

Or with explicit parameters:

```bash
python simulate_collapse.py --vram-limit-gb 8 --fill-stress-pct 1.20
```

### Useful flags

| Flag | Default | Description |
|------|---------|-------------|
| `--vram-limit-gb` | auto | Physical VRAM to target |
| `--matrix-dim` | 2048 | GEMM dimension (N×N) |
| `--iterations` | 50 | Benchmark iterations per phase |
| `--fill-baseline-pct` | 0.50 | VRAM fill ratio for baseline |
| `--fill-stress-pct` | 1.15 | VRAM fill ratio for stress (>1.0 = oversubscribe) |
| `--yes` | off | Skip confirmation prompt |

## Monitoring (run in a second terminal)

```bash
# GPU utilisation & memory — watch for utilisation drop during Phase 2
nvidia-smi dmon -s u -d 1

# Or the classic polling view
nvidia-smi -l 1
```

On Windows, also check **Task Manager → Performance → GPU** for the
"Shared GPU memory" counter spiking during Phase 2 — that's the paged
data sitting in system RAM.

## Key Takeaways

1. **`nvidia-smi` is the source of truth.**
   `torch.cuda.memory_reserved()` reflects PyTorch's caching allocator,
   not physical VRAM.  In our test, PyTorch reported 9.28 GB allocated
   on a 7.93 GB GPU — the delta was silently paged by WDDM.

2. **Admission Control is non-negotiable.**
   LLM runtimes must reject new requests *before* total KV-cache +
   model weights exceed **physical** VRAM.  Reactive eviction is too
   late — the first paged forward pass already destroys tail latency
   (P99 went from 3 ms to 169 ms for a single GEMM).

3. **Software cannot beat physics.**
   No kernel fusion, quantization, or attention variant recovers a
   68× bandwidth cliff caused by PCIe paging.  The only fix is to
   not page.

